        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> To be frank, this is a very rare scenario and I dont think most people will deal with such files.\r\n\r\nI am afraid this is indeed the case. But not only because it is a big file, but because it is *big enough* to cause delays but *not big enough* to enable preview's large file optimization.\r\n\r\nBuffer previews in 'mini.pick' are not done via simply showing the target buffer in a window (here is [an explanation why](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/d231729b13da28fd1625c3d85f2315886ddeb05d/lua/mini/pick.lua#L3093)). So they read necessary lines from the buffer and reapply highlighting *on every buffer lines preview*. Usually enabling highlighting is the most performance heavy part of this (and it is indeed true here also). To counter this, highlighting in preview is enabled [based on certain conditions](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/d231729b13da28fd1625c3d85f2315886ddeb05d/lua/mini/pick.lua#L3138-L3142), which pass in this case. In particular, the file is around 590 KiB (which is not enough for 1 MiB threshold) and there are only a handful of very long lines compared to the number of short ones (thus bypassing the 1000 bytes per line threshold). This particularly is indeed more visible when trying to preview lines closer to the end (because preview reads all lines from the start to have a more coherent highlighting).\r\n\r\nI don't really want to make preview highlighting threshold configurable. Mostly because it will also have to be done in 'mini.files' (as they share the \"preview buffer\" code) *and* this problem can mostly be worked around by navigating through the items in main (not preview) view.\r\n\r\nThere is also #489 for more information.\r\n\r\nI think I indeed will close this as a known limitation.",
